candidate diverging; it is named here so that does not repeat. Quote the pinned file, never the network, never a v0.5.0 line number.\\n\\nWHAT THIS LEAF MUST DELIVER. Create, attach, status, quiesce, safe-boundary, stop, stale termination and restore, through structured commands. Eight operations: report them as an explicit n-of-8 ratio with the production call site for each, and never let one operation's test stand in for another's.\\n\\n**READ THE FIRST LEAF BEFORE YOU WRITE A LINE.** `git -C .temp/STORY-260830-2t4g7i/worktree show --stat HEAD`, then `TASK-260830-35urbp_results.md`, its conformance matrix and `TASK-260830-35urbp_review-verdict-rev7.md`. That leaf shipped the decision core and argv only and **explicitly declared as YOUR bound** the things it did not build. Four handovers are waiting for you and each must be dispositioned in your results:\\n- **The exec and probe adapters do not exist.** `Dependencies` in the first leaf has no production implementation anywhere in the tree — nothing execs a server. Building them is this leaf's job, and with them comes the server socket's crash and idempotency evidence, or an explicit purity bound.\\n- **B18: the derived socket path is not checked against the platform `sun_path` limit** (104 bytes on macOS). A deep state root surfaces as a spawn failure in YOUR adapter. Decide it deliberately.\\n- **B16: an `ax` invocation from inside its own AX pane** (`TMUX=<derived>,<pid>,<idx>`) refuses `ambient collision` when the caller populates `Ambient` from the process environment. Over-strict rather than over-permissive, and the caller owns what it observes — but the first leaf's reviewer explicitly left the deliberate decision to you.\\n- **B19/B20: SPEC 810-812 requires rejecting a socket path, parent or ancestor whose ownership or permissions are unsafe BEFORE bind or connect.** The first leaf states the `localstore` layout owner for root creation custody and names YOUR bind step for the before-bind rejection. A 0777 root is admitted today on every first-leaf entry.\\n\\n**BUILD THE CENSUS FROM THE START. THIS IS THE SINGLE MOST EXPENSIVE LESSON OF THIS STORY.** The first leaf took SEVEN revisions, and every round after the second had the same shape: a rule witnessed at ONE of the several production entries that reach it, so a narrowing at any other entry walked through a green suite. The fix was not more tests, it was the instrument. Ship a **gate × entry census** as its own table in the conformance matrix, mirrored in TRACEABILITY: for every gate, list every production entry that can reach it, and give each cell exactly one of — a named committed test driving the gate THROUGH that entry plus an admitting narrowing row it kills; `unreachable` with the reason; or a stated BOUND naming what is unmeasured and who owns it. \\\"Pinned at the helper\\\" is not a cell: a helper pin plus an entry that reaches the same gate is two cells. For every gate implemented on two or more sides, compare the row sets side by side and report a symmetry line — the first leaf's last P2 was exactly a gate whose two sides had different row counts, and its own census showed the asymmetry without flagging it. Report the measured cell ratio.\\n\\nDETERMINISM IS A HARD CONSTRAINT AND IT SHAPES THE DESIGN. AGENTS.md requires tests that are deterministic and free of network or terminal dependencies. tmux 3.6a is installed on this host; that is NOT a licence to depend on it. Every rule must be driven to its effect by a committed test that passes with no tmux process anywhere. If you also write a test that talks to a real tmux server it must be separately gated and must NOT be the only witness for any acceptance row — state in the results which rows have a real-tmux witness in addition to their deterministic one. How you achieve that is your design decision; do not degrade a rule into a mock-only shape to make it testable, and do not ask. If a rule genuinely cannot be driven without a live server, say so as an e