{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000c465","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a0c7e8-b0ce-7b51-a856-c1e5aeba35b8"},"sequence":141,"recorded_at":1780531400000277,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"1e747257-7dba-4be5-8162-7ca370e28419","payload_type":"task.lifecycle.output","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"kind":"task_lifecycle","command_id":"1e747257-7dba-4be5-8162-7ca370e28419","run_stream":{"kind":"run","id":"1e747257-7dba-4be5-8162-7ca370e28419"},"task_stream":{"kind":"task","id":"01a0c7e9-15a5-7901-97d7-3140b31a993c"},"task_id":"01a0c7e9-15a5-7901-97d7-3140b31a993c","event":{"kind":"output","task_id":"01a0c7e9-15a5-7901-97d7-3140b31a993c","chunk":"{\n  \"chunk_id\": \"exec-5-1\",\n  \"command\": \"cat \\\"/Users/iv/Developer/ReluxWorks/agent-session-manager/.temp/resources/TASK-260830-1c28dz/TASK-260830-1c28dz_reviewer-cr2.md\\\"; echo \\\"=====REV3=====\\\"; cat \\\"/Users/iv/Developer/ReluxWorks/agent-session-manager/.temp/resources/TASK-260830-1c28dz/TASK-260830-1c28dz_rework-rev3.md\\\"\",\n  \"description\": \"Read reviewer CR2 and rework rev3\",\n  \"exit_code\": 0,\n  \"terminal_status\": \"completed\",\n  \"output\": \"Independently review Change Request revision 2 of TASK-260830-1c28dz (implement-tmux-lifecycle-operations), the SECOND of three leaves of STORY-260830-2t4g7i (production-tmux-backend). The chain is 35urbp -> 1c28dz -> g0pcnt; 35urbp (private tmux server management) is the signed checkpoint `1ba06e9` on the Story branch and g0pcnt carries the registry. Change Request shape: task_delta on the Story branch (trunk base 799c338; checkpointed predecessors 1ba06e9 ; tip 1ba06e99090e3b7bf1aa2f4865914f4080a644ed), published by RUN-260922-6d8a5c on muse-spark max, 34 changed paths, patch and validation log attached as TASK-260830-1c28dz_change-request_rev2.patch / _rev2-validation.log. Revisions 1..1 are earlier construction attempts of the same producer scope that failed the Change Request validation suite on the pre-PR51 10m race-test timeout under concurrent suites; they carry no review verdict and no prior review findings — review revision 2 on its own merits.. Read the producer brief TASK-260830-1c28dz_producer.md, the outcome TASK-260830-1c28dz_results.md, TASK-260830-1c28dz_conformance-matrix.md, TASK-260830-1c28dz_producer-evidence.tar.gz, the predecessor's accepted evidence (TASK-260830-35urbp_results.md and its verdict TASK-260830-35urbp_review-verdict-rev7.md), the CR patch and validation log, and the exact immutable CR bytes.\\n\\nNORMATIVE AUTHORITY: the pinned `internal/specdoc/SPEC.v0.7.0.md` **§4.2** (1403-1449, including the five-step after-restore sequence at 1433-1441) and **§3.2** (806-813: the runtime root, the socket path `<runtime>/tmux/ax.sock`, and component-by-component custody without following a symlink), with **§4.C** (1082), **§4.D** (1220), **§4.E** (1309) and **§4.4** (1478-1490) as context. The task record still names v0.5.0 and that is stale — a candidate citing a v0.5.0 line number or quoting text absent from the pinned file is a finding. Derive every expectation from the spec text, never from a production constant.\\n\\nVerify with your own instruments, on isolated immutable copies of the exact candidate tree; never mutate the live Story worktree, index, branch or HEAD; no product edits, commits, checkpoints or integration. Another Story is producing concurrently on this host, so record wall-clock and load for any gate you time.\\n\\n(1) **Eight operations, eight witnesses.** Create, attach, status, quiesce, safe-boundary, stop, stale termination, restore. Check each is driven through a production entry by a named committed test and that the results state the n-of-8 ratio with a call site per operation. One operation's test standing in for another's is a finding; so is an operation reported as driven whose test reaches only a helper.\\n\\n(2) **The after-restore sequence is ordered, and its order is the property.** §4.2 1433-1441: read the latest locally known lease; attempt a mesh lease refresh without blocking forever; resume locally ONLY under a winning local lease with valid materialization; offer remote attach/takeover when another host owns the session in an interactive terminal; otherwise enter `parked` WITHOUT launching the provider. Drive each branch and assert the outcome. Plant a reordering — an earlier step's refusal swallowing a later step's offer is exactly the defect a sibling Story shipped and had to repair, so check that the offer branch is reachable under a lapsed local grant, and that \\\"without blocking forever\\\" has a measured bound rather than a sentence.\\n\\n(3) **The four handovers from the first leaf.** That leaf shipped the decision core and argv only and named this leaf as owner for: the exec and probe adapters (its `Dependencies` had NO production implementation — verify they exist now and are driven, and that the server socket carries crash and idempotency evidence or an explicit purity bound); **B18** the `sun_path` limit (104 bytes on macOS) against the derived socket path; **B16** an `ax` invocation from inside its own AX pane refusing `ambient collision`; and **B19/B20** the SPEC 810-812 before-bind rejection of a socket path, parent or ancestor with unsafe ownership or permissions — a 0777 root is admitted today on every first-leaf entry, so check whether this leaf's bind step rejects it or states why not. Grade each as closed with its test, deferred with reason and owner, or silently dropped.\\n\\n(4) **The census is a deliverable, and its asymmetry is where to attack.** Verify the gate × entry census exists, that its counts reproduce mechanically, and that its per-entry kill attribution holds for the cells marked Measured. Then pick a gate it marks Measured at one entry and plant a narrowing at a DIFFERENT entry that reaches the same gate. Check the two-sided-gate symmetry lines: the predecessor's last P2 was a gate whose two sides carried different row counts, and its own census displayed the asymmetry without flagging it. A cell reading \\\"pinned at the helper\\\" and nothing else is the hole that cost that leaf six rounds.\\n\\n(5) **Determinism, verified not read.** Every rule must be driven to its effect by a committed test that passes with NO tmux process anywhere; any real-tmux test must be separately gated and never the only witness for an acceptance row. Verify by running the suite in an environment where the tmux binary cannot be resolved, and report exactly which tests fail or skip — a row whose only witness skips there is unmeasured however green the producer's log looks. Check the converse too: a rule degraded into a mock-only shape so it could be tested is a finding, not a solution; the production entry must still be the thing under test.\\n\\n(6) **No forked owners, and composition proven by driving.** `internal/tmuxserver` (the predecessor), `internal/terminalbackend`, `internal/axpane`, `internal/terminstance`, `internal/termbind`, `internal/secprim`, `internal/localstore`, `internal/fencing`, `internal/sessstate` are landed. Grep for a second model of anything they own. The results must name which existing type or gate was extended and which deliberately was not — verify that claim rather than accepting it. Then take outcome grids (not name-keyed per-test lists) over the complete importer set on the CR base and on the candidate and diff them; every class that moved must be named in the results with its before and after. If a landed test was changed to accept new behaviour, grade the argument and its spec citation — a landed regression test rewritten without one is a P1.\\n\\n(7) **Traceability boundary.** This leaf must NOT touch `internal/traceability` (registry, digest pin, README measured-coverage subsection) — that belongs to g0pcnt. Any edit there is a finding. A package-local TRACEABILITY.md and the conformance matrix are the right places.\\n\\n(8) **Mutation and hygiene.** Rerun the shipped harness in an isolated copy, rerun each KILLED twice, and plant at least four narrowings of your own on gates the producer did not mutate, plus an applied harmless SURVIVED control; per-plant raw logs, subprocess exits, precise denominators, NOT_APPLIED is not a pass. `PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE=1`, zero `__pycache__`/`.pyc` in the CR tree or your copies. gofmt/vet clean including `GOOS=windows GOARCH=amd64 go vet ./...` — the predecessor lost a round to a test file that reassigned a unix-only seam with no build constraint, and the local suite's `GOOS=windows go build` compiles no test files, so construction being green proves nothing there. The candidate byte-identical to trunk outside this Story's scope: check every path in `git diff --name-only <the CR base> <current origin/main>` that also appears in the candidate, because a refresh carries stale pre-refresh copies that read as reverts, and `task-board.config.json` must equal the base byte-for-byte. The full configured suite green on the exact tree; read the command count from the candidate config.\\n\\nReport the coverage ratio YOU measured against the acceptance table's real row count, not the producer's restated one. Attach TASK-260830-1c28dz_review-verdict-rev2.md and TASK-260830-1c28dz_review-evidence-rev2.tar.gz (a real gzip archive), complete the live merged checklist, then `accept_cr(TASK-260830-1c28dz, revision=2, evidence=TASK-260830-1c28dz_review-verdict-rev2.md)` or hand off changes requested with P1/P2/P3 classes named and a short \\\"Rework scope (for the producer)\\\" section at the end. Model: gpt-6-astra low. Canonical CLI /Users/iv/.curator/global/bin/task-board.\\n=====REV3=====\\nTHIS BRIEF SUPERSEDES TASK-260830-1c28dz_rework-rev2.md and every earlier precondition. Do exactly what is written here.\\nRework run for TASK-260830-1c28dz. Revision 2 was reviewed by RUN-260922-5091a4 (codex gpt-6-astra low) and routed CHANGES REQUESTED: **four P1**, three P2. Read TASK-260830-1c28dz_review-verdict-rev2.md in full first.\\n**Three of the five revision-1 P1s are confirmed fixed and stay fixed:** read-only attach now emits `-r` and the writable-vector defect is gone; `OSRunner` carries stderr and signal death as an error so unmarked, negative and permission probe failures no longer prove closure in the tested consumers; custody uses no-follow opens with a lexical ancestor walk and the intermediate-symlink refusal, identity seam and root/status, root/restore regressions all pass. B18, B16, the foreground fresh-decoy tests, the exec/probe/spawn adapters and the socket-unlink crash test also hold. Keep every one of them.\\n\\n**START WITH THE REFRESH — THE BRIEF'S OLD PREMISE IS NOW FALSE AND THAT IS MY DOING.** Every earlier brief on this leaf said \\\"trunk has NOT moved\\\". It has: I landed STORY-260917-kf9g1h while you were working, so `origin/main` is now **`40bb8c9f89a5430c556e8746e1dca9769f4b3c47`** and your HEAD is two commits behind it. Your candidate is still byte-identical to its checkpoint outside this leaf, but it differs from current main in sibling-owned `fencing`, `sessrepo`, `terminstance`, `axpane` and `traceability` files, and main-only tests are absent from your tree. So: run `task-board worktree refresh-candidate TASK-260830-1c28dz` FIRST, through the managed path — do not copy old sibling files over main and do not rebase the branch by hand. Then audit **every** path in `git diff --name-only 1ba06e9 40bb8c9` that also appears in your `git status`, and restore trunk content by hand for anything this leaf does not own: a refresh preserves your working candidate byte-for-byte, which is right for your files and wrong for files trunk changed underneath you, and those read to a reviewer as deliberate reverts. `task-board.config.json` must end byte-identical to the new base. After the refresh, re-run your package tests and the whole importer closure against the NEW dependencies before you touch any finding — a fix validated against two-commit-old siblings is not validated.\\n\\n1. **P1-A — a read-only observation reopens quiesced input.** `ops.go:130` records `StateActive` unconditionally after attach, and `checkAttachQuiesced` reads that same memory. So: quiesce succeeds, an admitted read-only attach flips the memory to active, and a subsequent input-authorized attach then succeeds with a writable `attach-session -t ...` argv. `TestReviewReadonlyMustNotReopenQuiescedInput` FAILS through three `Lifecycle.Execute` calls on your unmodified candidate — a sequential bypass, not a speculative race. §4.E 1383 and the §4.D input-quiescence semantics require that observation must not reopen input. Your new committed test checks the writable refusal BEFORE the read-only observation and stops short of the bypass. Preserve the barrier across observation, retry and recovery, and commit the full three-call sequence as a regression.\\n2. **P1-B — exact-instance status manufactures matching identity from the query.** `status.go:60-78` / `backend.ObserveStatus` sets `IdentityMatch` true and fills SessionID, ImplVersion, ProtoVersion and Generation **from the request**; `list-panes` supplies only command-name rows, so nothing independently observed establishes that the requested session and version own the instance. `TestReviewStatusMustObserveIdentityNotEchoQuery` records a valid binding, then queries the same present instance under a different session ID and under implementation version `9.9.9` — both return `identity_match:true`, parked, attachable. (The unrelated protocol-major case correctly refuses; keep it as the control, it is not a bug.) §4.C 1178-1187 requires all identity members to match and prescribes the absent/non-match result. Compose the durable binding and observed-identity owner instead of echoing the request into the engine's evidence.\\n3. **P1-C — reboot restore cannot produce a binding for the new server generation.** `ops.go:409-449` commits wrapper restoration first, then reads the CREATE-time binding and requires its generation to equal the new mutation context. `TestReviewRestoreAcrossServerGeneration` drives a successful production create, reopens the reboot-scoped receipt store using your own reboot convention, changes `CurrentGeneration` and the request to `generation-after-reboot`, and drives `Execute(restore)`: the runner receives `new-session` and then restore fails with `terminal_backend_integrity_failure at restore binding image`. §4.C 1211 requires prior binding, Checkpoint **and new binding** evidence; §4.E 1387 and §4.2 require restoration after reboot. Re-reading the old binding only solves the nil result inside the old generation. Compose the binding owner to persist and return the correct successor, validate before irreversible effects where you can, and prove retry and crash recovery across a real generation change.\\n4. **P1-D — the after-restore composition is still a report with no production consumer.** `ops.go:466-513` computes a second branch decision inside `tmuxserver`, returns `resumed_locally` / `remote_offered`, and nothing outside this package consumes either; the existing wrapper decision owner is not composed. Your own wire result is always parked, no provider is launched, and the tests assert strings rather than a resume or an offer effect. That does not close rev1 P1-E or the ordered-composition assignment for §4.2 1433-1441. And `TestExecuteRestoreRemoteOfferOnLapsedGrant` **does not lapse its grant** — it uses the standard future expiry; with an actually expired grant the reviewer's probe never reaches refresh or offer (`refreshed=false`, branch nil, unauthorized expiry). **That backend refusal is correct under the separate restore-authorization contract and MUST NOT be removed to make the probe pass** — it is the proof that the claimed lapsed-grant composition is absent at the entry you chose. Put the ordered decision at the correct wrapper/composition entry and drive the effects, including the lapsed-local-grant remote offer, without weakening backend authorization. Also: the callback receives a timeout context, but your \\\"blocking-refresh\\\" test neither measures elapsed duration nor drives a production mesh adapter — report the actual measured bound and the adapter assumptions honestly.\\n5. **P2-A — corrupt outcome reads become fresh successful evidence.** `lifecycle.go:477-500` treats ANY `LookupOutcome` error like absence and ignores `RecordOutcome` errors. `TestReviewCorruptOutcomeMustNotBecomeFreshEvidence` quiesces successfully, corrupts only the persisted report, advances the clock a second and retries the identical request: Execute returns success and moves `input_closed_at` from `12:00:00.000Z` to `12:00:01.000Z` **without closing input again**. This is not the B34 crash window you declared — an existing corrupt or unreadable report is silently replaced. §4.C 1207-1208 requires closure-time and generation evidence with stable retry proof. Keep failed reads distinct from missing reports, persist authoritative operation evidence with recovery semantics, and do not repair corruption by inventing a new event time.\\n6. **P2-B — five admitting narrowings still survive, and the comparison is still keyed on test verdicts.** Each leaves its gate present and admits exactly one class at one entry, and each survives the entire committed `tmuxserver` suite twice. Ship the missing refusal witnesses and their rows, reclassify the cells the census still calls unreachable, and give the side-by-side row counts the symmetry paragraph owes. Then replace the comparison: keying on test pass/fail verdicts is not an outcome grid, and the importer closure is 34 packages, not one. The sibling Story delivered exactly this and it was accepted — a runtime grid of actual return outcomes over the mechanically derived closure, with the instrumentation patches attached.\\n7. **Prose.** README's unconditional quiesce and replay claims must be corrected to match items 1 and 5. B33 already admits the wrapper never emits the boundary signal today and the timeout is fail-closed — keep that honest and do not describe it as a complete effect-level boundary protocol. B27 (production→Acquire composition) stays a bound, not a silent proof.\\n\\nPROCESS. Keep the candidate UNCOMMITTED; exactly one leaf's worth of delta on top of the checkpoint. Never commit on the Story branch, never edit `.task-board` directly, never self-accept, and do NOT touch `internal/traceability` — the final leaf g0pcnt carries the registry. Keep the zero-real-tmux determinism property: every rule driven to its effect with tmux unresolvable. After the refresh and the fixes, run your own package tests, the full importer closure, `GOOS=windows GOARCH=amd64 go vet ./...`, and the harness BEFORE handoff, then the full configured suite once from `spawn.worktree_isolation.validation.commands` at the NEW base. Update the results with a finding-by-finding table, the refresh audit (every path in `git diff --name-only 1ba06e9 40bb8c9` that intersected your candidate and what you did with it), the reclassified census with its ratio and symmetry counts, and the runtime importer-closure grid; update the conformance matrix and the evidence archive, attach the results standalone, verify every attachment by reading it back off the board, then `task-board handoff TASK-260830-1c28dz --role developer` so revision 3 is constructed, and exit promptly. Hand off no later than 3.5 hours in; if the four P1s cannot all be closed, hand off a reviewable slice and enumerate precisely what remains. Canonical CLI /Users/iv/.curator/global/bin/task-board. Model: muse-spark max. The independent reviewer will be codex gpt-6-astra low.\\nREJECTION PATTERNS THE INDEPENDENT REVIEWER HAS ALREADY CAUGHT ON THIS EPIC (2026-09-17: TASK-260830-1geqhj rev1, TASK-260830-24z2b3 rev2, TASK-260830-219okr rev3 were all routed CHANGES REQUESTED, and between them these six shapes account for every finding). Read this as a checklist you run against your own work BEFORE handoff; each item names what a reviewer will do to your candidate.\\n(a) Circular expectations. An assertion that compares a production value against a production constant (`got.Code == pkg.SomeUnsupportedCode`) pins nothing: swapping the constant to another registered value with the same exit keeps the suite green. Assert the LITERAL token from the pinned specification text, at the production entry, and make sure the literal actually occurs in a committed test — not only in a comment. Derive every expectation from the spec, never from the implementation.\\n(b) A rule pinned at the helper instead of the entry. A test that calls the inner helper directly proves the helper; it says nothing about whether the composed production entry still reaches it. Drive every rule through the entry point the conformance matrix names as its call site, and if a rule is implemented TWICE (a Build path and a Decode path, an exported entry and its internal sibling), each side needs its own narrowing mutant — a rule pinned only on one side leaves the other side unmeasured, and reviewers plant exactly there.\\n(c) Forking a landed gate. Never re-implement what internal/environ, internal/canonicaljson, internal/scalar or another landed owner already gates (string bounds in CHARACTERS not bytes, uint53, digests, UUIDv7, timestamps, sorted-unique arrays, closed-shape decoding, the AX number model that forbids floating point, values at or beyond 2^53 and rounding-and-continuing). Delegate to it and keep only your package-specific rules. Every fork this epic produced became a defect: a byte-measured bound, an extension value rounded at 2^60, nested duplicate members collapsed by a lenient decode.\\n(d) A census or an inventory standing in for behaviour. A reflection census of field NAMES does not prove that a cached observation cannot authorize; an arm inventory keyed by message text does not prove a condition edit is caught. Drive the input, assert the effect.\\n(e) Mutant rows that do not measure what they claim. An arm-delete (`&& false`) is not a narrowing; a row patching two sites whose kill comes from one is one measured site reported as two; a plant with no raw log and no subprocess exit is a verdict line, not evidence. Ship one raw log per plant per run, state narrowing vs arm-delete per row, and include an applied harmless SURVIVED control so the harness is proven able to report a survivor.\\n(f) Prose that outruns the tests. \\\"Composes X\\\", \\\"never admits what decoding refuses\\\", \\\"nothing re-implemented\\\", \\\"no cached sentinel can authorize\\\" are censusable claims: a reviewer greps for the callers and plants the counter-example. Before you write a sentence like that in a doc comment, README, LOGBOOK or TRACEABILITY row, make a test fail without the property. If you cannot, state it as an explicit BOUND instead, naming what is not covered.\\nFinally: report the coverage ratio you MEASURED, not the one you planned. Two of the three rejected revisions claimed a complete ratio and the reviewer measured a smaller one; an honest smaller number with named gaps is accepted, an inflated one is a finding.\\n\",\n  \"original_output_bytes\": 21911,\n  \"original_output_tokens\": 5478,\n  \"truncated\": false\n}","final_result":true}}}
