{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000c497","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a0c7e8-b0ce-7b51-a856-c1e5aeba35b8"},"sequence":166,"recorded_at":1780531400000327,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"1e747257-7dba-4be5-8162-7ca370e28419","payload_type":"task.lifecycle.output","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"kind":"task_lifecycle","command_id":"1e747257-7dba-4be5-8162-7ca370e28419","run_stream":{"kind":"run","id":"1e747257-7dba-4be5-8162-7ca370e28419"},"task_stream":{"kind":"task","id":"01a0c7e9-209f-7f72-9490-d7f6b071bc02"},"task_id":"01a0c7e9-209f-7f72-9490-d7f6b071bc02","event":{"kind":"output","task_id":"01a0c7e9-209f-7f72-9490-d7f6b071bc02","chunk":"{\n  \"chunk_id\": \"exec-6-1\",\n  \"command\": \"cat \\\"/Users/iv/Developer/ReluxWorks/agent-session-manager/.temp/resources/TASK-260830-1c28dz/TASK-260830-1c28dz_reviewer-cr3.md\\\"; echo \\\"=====REV4=====\\\"; cat \\\"/Users/iv/Developer/ReluxWorks/agent-session-manager/.temp/resources/TASK-260830-1c28dz/TASK-260830-1c28dz_rework-rev4.md\\\"\",\n  \"description\": \"Read reviewer CR3 and rework rev4\",\n  \"exit_code\": 0,\n  \"terminal_status\": \"completed\",\n  \"output\": \"Independently review Change Request revision 3 of TASK-260830-1c28dz (implement-tmux-lifecycle-operations), the SECOND of three leaves of STORY-260830-2t4g7i (production-tmux-backend). The chain is 35urbp -> 1c28dz -> g0pcnt; 35urbp (private tmux server management) is the signed checkpoint `1ba06e9` on the Story branch and g0pcnt carries the registry. Change Request shape: task_delta on the Story branch (trunk base 40bb8c9; checkpointed predecessors d4bd91d ; tip d4bd91d0e740285b21b8d65bf6f074c8009b04ae), published by RUN-260922-b3945b on muse-spark max, 41 changed paths, patch and validation log attached as TASK-260830-1c28dz_change-request_rev3.patch / _rev3-validation.log. Revisions 1..2 are earlier construction attempts of the same producer scope that failed the Change Request validation suite on the pre-PR51 10m race-test timeout under concurrent suites; they carry no review verdict and no prior review findings — review revision 3 on its own merits.. Read the producer brief TASK-260830-1c28dz_producer.md, the outcome TASK-260830-1c28dz_results.md, TASK-260830-1c28dz_conformance-matrix.md, TASK-260830-1c28dz_producer-evidence.tar.gz, the predecessor's accepted evidence (TASK-260830-35urbp_results.md and its verdict TASK-260830-35urbp_review-verdict-rev7.md), the CR patch and validation log, and the exact immutable CR bytes.\\n\\nNORMATIVE AUTHORITY: the pinned `internal/specdoc/SPEC.v0.7.0.md` **§4.2** (1403-1449, including the five-step after-restore sequence at 1433-1441) and **§3.2** (806-813: the runtime root, the socket path `<runtime>/tmux/ax.sock`, and component-by-component custody without following a symlink), with **§4.C** (1082), **§4.D** (1220), **§4.E** (1309) and **§4.4** (1478-1490) as context. The task record still names v0.5.0 and that is stale — a candidate citing a v0.5.0 line number or quoting text absent from the pinned file is a finding. Derive every expectation from the spec text, never from a production constant.\\n\\nREVISION HISTORY, stated so no template sentence misleads you: revision 1 was routed CHANGES REQUESTED with five P1; revision 2 with four P1 and three P2; three of the revision-1 P1s (read-only attach argv, OSRunner probe classification, custody no-follow) are confirmed fixed and must stay fixed. Read the numbered verdict for the revision you are reviewing and the one before it before grading anything as closed.\\n\\nVerify with your own instruments, on isolated immutable copies of the exact candidate tree; never mutate the live Story worktree, index, branch or HEAD; no product edits, commits, checkpoints or integration. Another Story is producing concurrently on this host, so record wall-clock and load for any gate you time.\\n\\n(1) **Eight operations, eight witnesses.** Create, attach, status, quiesce, safe-boundary, stop, stale termination, restore. Check each is driven through a production entry by a named committed test and that the results state the n-of-8 ratio with a call site per operation. One operation's test standing in for another's is a finding; so is an operation reported as driven whose test reaches only a helper.\\n\\n(2) **The after-restore sequence is ordered, and its order is the property.** §4.2 1433-1441: read the latest locally known lease; attempt a mesh lease refresh without blocking forever; resume locally ONLY under a winning local lease with valid materialization; offer remote attach/takeover when another host owns the session in an interactive terminal; otherwise enter `parked` WITHOUT launching the provider. Drive each branch and assert the outcome. Plant a reordering — an earlier step's refusal swallowing a later step's offer is exactly the defect a sibling Story shipped and had to repair, so check that the offer branch is reachable under a lapsed local grant, and that \\\"without blocking forever\\\" has a measured bound rather than a sentence.\\n\\n(3) **The four handovers from the first leaf.** That leaf shipped the decision core and argv only and named this leaf as owner for: the exec and probe adapters (its `Dependencies` had NO production implementation — verify they exist now and are driven, and that the server socket carries crash and idempotency evidence or an explicit purity bound); **B18** the `sun_path` limit (104 bytes on macOS) against the derived socket path; **B16** an `ax` invocation from inside its own AX pane refusing `ambient collision`; and **B19/B20** the SPEC 810-812 before-bind rejection of a socket path, parent or ancestor with unsafe ownership or permissions — a 0777 root is admitted today on every first-leaf entry, so check whether this leaf's bind step rejects it or states why not. Grade each as closed with its test, deferred with reason and owner, or silently dropped.\\n\\n(4) **The census is a deliverable, and its asymmetry is where to attack.** Verify the gate × entry census exists, that its counts reproduce mechanically, and that its per-entry kill attribution holds for the cells marked Measured. Then pick a gate it marks Measured at one entry and plant a narrowing at a DIFFERENT entry that reaches the same gate. Check the two-sided-gate symmetry lines: the predecessor's last P2 was a gate whose two sides carried different row counts, and its own census displayed the asymmetry without flagging it. A cell reading \\\"pinned at the helper\\\" and nothing else is the hole that cost that leaf six rounds.\\n\\n(5) **Determinism, verified not read.** Every rule must be driven to its effect by a committed test that passes with NO tmux process anywhere; any real-tmux test must be separately gated and never the only witness for an acceptance row. Verify by running the suite in an environment where the tmux binary cannot be resolved, and report exactly which tests fail or skip — a row whose only witness skips there is unmeasured however green the producer's log looks. Check the converse too: a rule degraded into a mock-only shape so it could be tested is a finding, not a solution; the production entry must still be the thing under test.\\n\\n(6) **No forked owners, and composition proven by driving.** `internal/tmuxserver` (the predecessor), `internal/terminalbackend`, `internal/axpane`, `internal/terminstance`, `internal/termbind`, `internal/secprim`, `internal/localstore`, `internal/fencing`, `internal/sessstate` are landed. Grep for a second model of anything they own. The results must name which existing type or gate was extended and which deliberately was not — verify that claim rather than accepting it. Then take outcome grids (not name-keyed per-test lists) over the complete importer set on the CR base and on the candidate and diff them; every class that moved must be named in the results with its before and after. If a landed test was changed to accept new behaviour, grade the argument and its spec citation — a landed regression test rewritten without one is a P1.\\n\\n(7) **Traceability boundary.** This leaf must NOT touch `internal/traceability` (registry, digest pin, README measured-coverage subsection) — that belongs to g0pcnt. Any edit there is a finding. A package-local TRACEABILITY.md and the conformance matrix are the right places.\\n\\n(8) **Mutation and hygiene.** Rerun the shipped harness in an isolated copy, rerun each KILLED twice, and plant at least four narrowings of your own on gates the producer did not mutate, plus an applied harmless SURVIVED control; per-plant raw logs, subprocess exits, precise denominators, NOT_APPLIED is not a pass. `PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE=1`, zero `__pycache__`/`.pyc` in the CR tree or your copies. gofmt/vet clean including `GOOS=windows GOARCH=amd64 go vet ./...` — the predecessor lost a round to a test file that reassigned a unix-only seam with no build constraint, and the local suite's `GOOS=windows go build` compiles no test files, so construction being green proves nothing there. The candidate byte-identical to trunk outside this Story's scope: check every path in `git diff --name-only <the CR base> <current origin/main>` that also appears in the candidate, because a refresh carries stale pre-refresh copies that read as reverts, and `task-board.config.json` must equal the base byte-for-byte. The full configured suite green on the exact tree; read the command count from the candidate config.\\n\\nReport the coverage ratio YOU measured against the acceptance table's real row count, not the producer's restated one. Attach TASK-260830-1c28dz_review-verdict-rev3.md and TASK-260830-1c28dz_review-evidence-rev3.tar.gz (a real gzip archive), complete the live merged checklist, then `accept_cr(TASK-260830-1c28dz, revision=3, evidence=TASK-260830-1c28dz_review-verdict-rev3.md)` or hand off changes requested with P1/P2/P3 classes named and a short \\\"Rework scope (for the producer)\\\" section at the end. Model: gpt-6-astra low. Canonical CLI /Users/iv/.curator/global/bin/task-board.\\n=====REV4=====\\nTHIS BRIEF SUPERSEDES TASK-260830-1c28dz_rework-rev3.md and every earlier precondition. Do exactly what is written here.\\nRework run for TASK-260830-1c28dz. Revision 3 was reviewed by RUN-260922-f640b1 (codex gpt-6-astra low) and routed CHANGES REQUESTED: **three P1**, two P2. Read TASK-260830-1c28dz_review-verdict-rev3.md first. The managed refresh onto `40bb8c9` was done correctly and the base is now `d4bd91d0`; the rev2 findings you closed stay closed.\\n**The three P1s are one family, and naming it will save you a round.** Each is a composition that authorizes something neither of its parts would: a failed durable write leaves an operation reporting success; two gates that each pass authorize *different leases*; a refresh whose deadline elapsed is accepted because only the returned error was checked. In every case the individual component behaves as written. Stop testing components and test the joins.\\n\\n1. **P1-A — quiescence fails open after its state write fails.** `lifecycle.go:410-426` commits the engine operation and then calls `recordAfter`, and `recordAfter` at line 242 **ignores `States.Record` errors**; `ops.go:156-167` enforces the writable-attach refusal only from that supposedly advisory memory, so its absence admits input. `TestReviewQuiesceStateWriteFailureCannotReopenInput` drives the unmodified production `Lifecycle.Execute` twice: an `AfterStage` failure for the instance document leaves quiesce returning **success**, and once the hook is removed a writable attach succeeds with `attach-session -t ...` and no `-r` — after the runner has already executed `lock-session` and `detach-client`. This is a durable-state failure bypass of the input barrier; it is neither the read-only-attach sequence you already fixed nor the B34 report-time window you declared. §4.C quiesce and §4.E input closure both forbid it. Persist and recover the enforcing barrier authoritatively: a failed write must never produce successful closure followed by writable admission.\\n2. **P1-B — the refreshed lease decision is not bound to the operation it executes.** `wrapper.go:114-139` decides over the refreshed winner and then passes the independently supplied `req.Restore` into `Lifecycle.Execute`; backend authorization consults `lc.CurrentLease()` again, and **nothing ties the two together**. `TestReviewRefreshedWinnerMustBindExecutedRestore` refreshes to local lease B at epoch 9 and presents that tuple to the wrapper, while the backend request and `CurrentLease` still carry lease A at epoch 7: `ExecuteWrapperRestore` returns `ActionLaunch` with a nil error and executes `new-session` under the **old** authorization. Two gates pass and their composition authorizes different leases. §4.2 step 3 requires the winning local lease, not two independent successes. Bind the selected winner, the decision identity and materialization, and the effect request into one authorization; reconcile or refuse divergent observations before execution — and do not weaken backend restore authorization to make it fit.\\n3. **P1-C — an expired refresh is accepted as verified, and your advertised bound is cooperative only.** `wrapper.go:168-179` calls the callback synchronously, cancels its context and checks only the returned error; it never asks whether the deadline elapsed before the answer arrived. `TestReviewExpiredRefreshMustNotResume` waits for `ctx.Done()` inside the callback and then returns a local winner with a nil error — the unmodified entry reports `RefreshSucceeded=true`, selects launch and executes `new-session`, bypassing your documented timed-out fallback. And `TestReviewRefreshBoundNotJustCooperativeDeadline` gives the entry a 1 ms bound with a callback returning after 100 ms: the entry blocks for **102.575 ms**. That finite probe does not claim to have measured an infinite wait; it shows the entry does not enforce the bound it advertises. No production `LeaseRefresh` adapter exists outside this function-field interface, and your committed test uses a cooperative callback while allowing five seconds for a 50 ms setting. Reject expired and cancelled results, and either provide the bounded adapter and composition or state and prove the cooperative contract accurately. §4.2 step 2's non-blocking requirement and your comments calling the bound \\\"not advisory\\\" are not established by a context deadline alone.\\n4. **P2-A — the importer outcome comparison is still absent, asked for the third time. Here is the exact shape, since describing it has not worked.** Your `outcome_compare.py` keys `out[ev[\\\"Test\\\"]] = ev[\\\"Action\\\"]` — it does not even include Package — and the results then claim retained test passes prove no landed behaviour changed. Test verdicts are not admission outcomes. Build this instead: (a) derive the closure mechanically with `go list -json ./...` over the nine assigned composing packages — the reviewer gets 34 production packages, 36 including the test-only importers `crashgate` and `environ`; (b) instrument the **production entries** of the packages your change can reach so each observed call emits one record of `{package, entry, input class, returned outcome class, refusal code if any}`; (c) run the same fixed input corpus against the CR base and against the candidate, under identical seeds and clocks; (d) diff the two streams **keyed on `(package, entry, input class)` with the outcome class as the value**, and list every key whose outcome class changed, with its before and after; (e) attach the instrumentation patches and both raw streams. A key that appears only on one side is also a moved class. Existing pass/fail inventories may stay as supplementary material, clearly labelled as such.\\n5. **P2-B — the census arithmetic and the traceability prose are wrong in five specific places.** The 119×21 Table D recount (167 M, 152 B, 2180 U) is correct and the reclassification of reachable-undriven cells to B39 is accepted as an honest limitation. But: Table C lists 18 `U2c` gate names while claiming 19; tables A/B/C/D contain 264+504+1309+2499 = **4576** cells, not the claimed 5576, and the category totals also sum to 4576 (227+156+4193); row 83 still names five removed tests (`TestExecuteRestoreRemoteOfferOnLapsedGrant`, `...OffersWhenRemoteHoldsLocalTuple`, `...ResumesUnderLocalLease`, `...ParksOtherwise`, `...RefreshBounded`), and the same stale row is in the attached matrix; row 83 and the results say successor persistence precedes effects, but in production `executeRestore` calls `executeWithReceipt` at ops.go:430 and only then `restoreBinding` at 437, so **prior validation** precedes effects while successor persistence does not — keep that distinction explicit; and the traceability introduction still says 91/91 over rows 59-91 while the table now has row 92, and the results still say no test reads wall time, which your own new elapsed-time wrapper test contradicts. Fix each, and recount rather than restating.\\n6. **Four more clause-level admitting narrowings survive your committed suite twice**, none of them in your shipped harness, each leaving its gate and the gate's other refusal members intact — including `list-sessions` exiting 1 with plausible stdout, and exactly `review-foreign-instance` accepted as a matching attached-count row, both through `Execute(status) → probeAttached`. Read the reviewer's table, ship a row per plant, and name the committed test that kills each.\\n\\nPROCESS. Keep the candidate UNCOMMITTED; exactly one leaf's worth of delta on top of the checkpoint. Base is `d4bd91d0`; if trunk moves again, refresh through the managed path and repeat the path-by-path audit. Never commit on the Story branch, never edit `.task-board` directly, never self-accept, and do NOT touch `internal/traceability` — the final leaf g0pcnt carries the registry. Keep the zero-real-tmux determinism property. Run your own package tests, the full importer closure, `GOOS=windows GOARCH=amd64 go vet ./...`, and the harness BEFORE handoff, then the full configured suite once from `spawn.worktree_isolation.validation.commands`. Update the results with a finding-by-finding table, the recounted census, and the outcome grid from item 4 with every moved key named; update the conformance matrix and the evidence archive, attach the results standalone, verify every attachment by reading it back off the board, then `task-board handoff TASK-260830-1c28dz --role developer` so revision 4 is constructed, and exit promptly. Hand off no later than 3.5 hours in; if the three P1s cannot all be closed, hand off a reviewable slice and enumerate precisely what remains. Canonical CLI /Users/iv/.curator/global/bin/task-board. Model: muse-spark max. The independent reviewer will be codex gpt-6-astra low.\\nREJECTION PATTERNS THE INDEPENDENT REVIEWER HAS ALREADY CAUGHT ON THIS EPIC (2026-09-17: TASK-260830-1geqhj rev1, TASK-260830-24z2b3 rev2, TASK-260830-219okr rev3 were all routed CHANGES REQUESTED, and between them these six shapes account for every finding). Read this as a checklist you run against your own work BEFORE handoff; each item names what a reviewer will do to your candidate.\\n(a) Circular expectations. An assertion that compares a production value against a production constant (`got.Code == pkg.SomeUnsupportedCode`) pins nothing: swapping the constant to another registered value with the same exit keeps the suite green. Assert the LITERAL token from the pinned specification text, at the production entry, and make sure the literal actually occurs in a committed test — not only in a comment. Derive every expectation from the spec, never from the implementation.\\n(b) A rule pinned at the helper instead of the entry. A test that calls the inner helper directly proves the helper; it says nothing about whether the composed production entry still reaches it. Drive every rule through the entry point the conformance matrix names as its call site, and if a rule is implemented TWICE (a Build path and a Decode path, an exported entry and its internal sibling), each side needs its own narrowing mutant — a rule pinned only on one side leaves the other side unmeasured, and reviewers plant exactly there.\\n(c) Forking a landed gate. Never re-implement what internal/environ, internal/canonicaljson, internal/scalar or another landed owner already gates (string bounds in CHARACTERS not bytes, uint53, digests, UUIDv7, timestamps, sorted-unique arrays, closed-shape decoding, the AX number model that forbids floating point, values at or beyond 2^53 and rounding-and-continuing). Delegate to it and keep only your package-specific rules. Every fork this epic produced became a defect: a byte-measured bound, an extension value rounded at 2^60, nested duplicate members collapsed by a lenient decode.\\n(d) A census or an inventory standing in for behaviour. A reflection census of field NAMES does not prove that a cached observation cannot authorize; an arm inventory keyed by message text does not prove a condition edit is caught. Drive the input, assert the effect.\\n(e) Mutant rows that do not measure what they claim. An arm-delete (`&& false`) is not a narrowing; a row patching two sites whose kill comes from one is one measured site reported as two; a plant with no raw log and no subprocess exit is a verdict line, not evidence. Ship one raw log per plant per run, state narrowing vs arm-delete per row, and include an applied harmless SURVIVED control so the harness is proven able to report a survivor.\\n(f) Prose that outruns the tests. \\\"Composes X\\\", \\\"never admits what decoding refuses\\\", \\\"nothing re-implemented\\\", \\\"no cached sentinel can authorize\\\" are censusable claims: a reviewer greps for the callers and plants the counter-example. Before you write a sentence like that in a doc comment, README, LOGBOOK or TRACEABILITY row, make a test fail without the property. If you cannot, state it as an explicit BOUND instead, naming what is not covered.\\nFinally: report the coverage ratio you MEASURED, not the one you planned. Two of the three rejected revisions claimed a complete ratio and the reviewer measured a smaller one; an honest smaller number with named gaps is accepted, an inflated one is a finding.\\n\",\n  \"original_output_bytes\": 21059,\n  \"original_output_tokens\": 5265,\n  \"truncated\": false\n}","final_result":true}}}
